> x is a byte
  note: "byte" without signed or unsigned: read as unsigned, u8, not signed, i8 (say "unsigned byte", or "assume bytes are signed")
  ok: from now on x is a byte
> c is a char
  note: "char" without signed or unsigned: read as signed, i8 (as on x86), not unsigned, u8 (as on ARM) (say "signed char", or "assume chars are unsigned")
  ok: from now on c is a char
> x is an int
  note: "int": read as a C int, i32, not an unbounded integer, int (say "32-bit int" or "unbounded integer", or "assume int means unbounded")
  ok: from now on x is an int
> n is an integer
  note: "integer" without a width: read as an unbounded integer, int, not a C int, i32 (say "32-bit integer", or "assume integers are 32-bit")
  ok: from now on n is an integer
> x and y
_(x: real, y: real) = x != 0 && y != 0
  -> bool
> the bitwise and of x and y
  ucas: it(x: i32, y) = x & y
  ucas: show it
  note: y has no type: inferred int from the integer operations in the body
it(x: i32, y: int) = int(x) & y
  -> int
> the bitwise not of x
  ucas: it(x: i32) = ~x
  ucas: show it
it(x: i32) = ~x
  -> i32
